nd  14 days so you have this you know almost  two weeks to spread the virus around  after that yeah sure it'd be useful but  again there's a big difference between  trying to push a system a permanent  policy for this temporary crisis and or  putting in place a temporary measure and  the other problem with with this by the  way is if you don't target it to those  who do not have paid leave what you're  going to end up having is a system where  effectively you're shifting the  responsibility of those companies who  were providing paid leave privately to  the taxpayers because as we've seen in  states that have adopted paid leave  policy those companies usually starts  telling their employees to first use the  state-based policy and then they'll fill  the gap with theirs yeah this just seems  like an example of what you were talking  about before which is that people are  using this too as an opportunity to push  policies that they already believed in  in some cases that might make sense as  with in in terms of trade and that being  a temporary jolt to eliminate tariffs  but it seems like what we really need to  do is solve the public health problem  and and treat this as a public health  problem first and to that extent you  know a temporary  emergency provision of sick leave to  those who don't already have it could  help to some extent veronique thank you  so much for speaking with me today  you're welcome  thank you for having me

